We don't have freeways,

00:40:25.323 --> 00:40:28.917 position:63%
 you don't have on-ramps and off-ramps
and all that weird sh*t.

00:40:28.984 --> 00:40:30.161 position:63%
Traffic.

00:40:30.228 --> 00:40:33.364 position:63%
-Yeah, out here I was driving like,
I don't know,

00:40:33.431 --> 00:40:35.933 position:63%
15, 20 minutes without seeing a car.

00:40:36.000 --> 00:40:37.041 position:63%
I don't even own…

00:40:37.108 --> 00:40:39.770 position:63%
You can drive all day
without seeing a car sometimes.

00:40:39.837 --> 00:40:42.630 position:63%
Here, down two miles and back to here.

00:40:42.697 --> 00:40:45.376 position:63%
-What do people do for work?
Just ranching or?

00:40:45.443 --> 00:40:48.646 position:63%
Well, there's ranching.
There's firewood getting for some people.

00:40:48.713 --> 00:40:50.848 position:63%
There's logging outfits.

00:40:50.915 --> 00:40:53.359 position:63%
-Sometimes you want to get with.
-Heavy equipment stuff.

00:40:53.426 --> 00:40:54.859 position:63%
Some kind of stuff.

00:40:54.926 --> 00:40:58.431 position:63%
There's a reman Lumberyard in Oroville.

00:40:58.498 --> 00:41:01.208 position:63%
-That's the big employer there.
-The lumberyard?

00:41:01.275 --> 00:41:03.294 position:63%
-Yeah. Lot of lumber.
-Okay.

00:41:03.361 --> 00:41:09.134 position:63%
Canada sends down rough cut stuff
so they don't have to pay tariffs on it.

00:41:09.634 --> 00:41:11.702 position:63%
-Okay. How's that work? What?
-They send rough cut.

00:41:11.769 --> 00:41:15.006 position:63%
They send two-by rough cut down here.
Don't pay tariffs on it.

00:41:15.073 --> 00:41:17.540 position:63%
And here they process it into one-by.

00:41:18.176 --> 00:41:20.378 position:63%
-I thought the tariffs
were bad for that right now.

00:41:20.445 --> 00:41:23.095 position:63%
Well, finished lumber, yeah.
Rough cut, nope.

00:41:23.162 --> 00:41:25.450 position:63%
Because it's not a finished product.

00:41:25.517 --> 00:41:26.517 position:63%
-Interesting.

00:41:26.584 --> 00:41:28.786 position:63%
So they bring it down here
and then they finish it here.

00:41:28.853 --> 00:41:32.356 position:63%
Now how much of it goes back up there?
I don't know but it goes out all over.

00:41:32.423 --> 00:41:34.959 position:63%
-Gotcha. So that industry
is still thriving up here?

00:41:35.026 --> 00:41:36.527 position:63%
A lot of the logging mills…

00:41:36.594 --> 00:41:39.597 position:63%
There used to be another one in town
but they're no longer.

00:41:39.664 --> 00:41:42.864 position:63%
I don't know how long
they've been out, 10 years.

00:41:42.931 --> 00:41:44.794 position:63%
And now they just have piles.

00:41:44.861 --> 00:41:48.205 position:63%
Can't even get rid of them hardly
because there's so much competition.

00:41:48.272 --> 00:41:50.207 position:63%
And they ship stuff out on a train.

00:41:50.274 --> 00:41:53.144 position:63%
Or they fill trains up
with like chips or whatever

00:41:53.211 --> 00:41:58.215 position:63%
and send that off somewhere else
to be turned into like OSB or whatever.

00:41:58.282 --> 00:42:01.290 position:63%
So it's kind of like,
nothing gets wasted out here.

00:42:01.357 --> 00:42:02.386 position:63%
-Right. Right.

00:42:02.453 --> 00:42:05.423 position:63%
But yeah, I mean that's
the biggest employer around.

00:42:05.490 --> 00:42:07.297 position:63%
You know, for a company.

00:42:07.364 --> 00:42:10.194 position:63%
-Which way did you come from?
-Man, I zigzagged it so hard.

00:42:10.261 --> 00:42:13.564 position:63%
I came from Tonasket.
I took the long way.

00:42:13.631 --> 00:42:16.434 position:63%
I was on dirt road.
Ended up on someone's house.

00:42:16.501 --> 00:42:18.098 position:63%
On the end of a road.

00:42:18.165 --> 00:42:21.171 position:63%
And then I… And then I went up to Molson.

00:42:21.238 --> 00:42:22.073 position:63%
Okay.

00:42:22.140 --> 00:42:26.459 position:63%
-Chesaw is the only town around
that's named after an Asian dude.

00:42:26.526 --> 00:42:29.246 position:63%
Only town in Washington
named after a Chinese dude.

00:42:29.313 --> 00:42:31.649 position:63%
-I read the plaque out there.
He married some Native lady.

00:42:31.716 --> 00:42:34.986 position:63%
-Married to a Native gal.
It was still tribal back then.

00:42:35.053 --> 00:42:36.386 position:63%
-Yeah?
-This was all…

00:42:36.886 --> 00:42:39.194 position:63%
Okanogan County was all tribal.

00:42:45.596 --> 00:42:49.166 position:63%
They said the big event here
is the Fourth of July rodeo.

00:42:49.233 --> 00:42:52.942 position:63%
Thousands of people
come from around the country.

00:42:53.471 --> 00:42:59.254 position:63%
There are no hotels here so they open up
the fields for camping and for RVs.

00:42:59.743 --> 00:43:03.148 position:63%
And all of these stands fill up here.

00:43:04.382 --> 00:43:07.395 position:63%
With fans and celebration.

00:43:08.186 --> 00:43:09.719 position:63%
And I'm sure fireworks.

00:43:10.788 --> 00:43:14.759 position:63%
All in this pristine, remote.

00:43:14.826 --> 00:43:17.092 position:63%
I mean I'm talking remote, remote.

00:43:18.432 --> 00:43:21.623 position:63%
Far out there part of the state.

00:43:22.533 --> 00:43:26.637 position:63%
And other than that,
I think they just stand empty like this.

00:43:26.704 --> 00:43:29.847 position:63%
Waiting for another
Fourth of July to come.

00:43:31.114 --> 00:43:33.610 position:63%
[Western music]

00:43:54.581 --> 00:43:57.368 position:63%
[Peter] All right, guys,
we're going up to Joel's ranch.

00:43:57.435 --> 00:44:02.606 position:63%
I'm looking forward to getting a good
ranching perspective on things 'cause

00:44:02.680 --> 00:44:05.511 position:63%
out in these parts there is no way
you're gonna run into one,

00:44:05.578 --> 00:44:07.611 position:63%
at least on a day like today.

00:44:08.379 --> 00:44:12.483 position:63%
I think this is his place. Hope so.

00:44:13.217 --> 00:44:18.488 position:63%
Because I can imagine getting met with
some firearms out here for trespassing.

00:44:21.325 --> 00:44:24.582 position:63%
So from when we started today
to right now,

00:44:25.129 --> 00:44:26.662 position:63%
eight and a half hours.

00:44:27.197 --> 00:44:28.468 position:63%
That's how long it's been.

00:44:28.535 --> 00:44:31.535 position:63%
Like, it's been most of the time
just driving.

00:44:33.170 --> 00:44:34.720 position:63%
So the distances,

00:44:34.787 --> 00:44:37.575 position:63%
which you're not going to understand
through the video camera…

00:44:37.642 --> 00:44:39.242 position:63%
are so freaking massive.

00:44:39.744 --> 00:44:42.028 position:63%
It's impossible to put into words

00:44:42.095 --> 00:44:46.383 position:63%
or show in a video like this
'cause you're seeing the edited version.

00:44:46.450 --> 00:44:49.420 position:63%
But it's just been a full day.

00:44:49.487 --> 00:44:53.336 position:63%
I thought I'd be here at like 12:00,
and it's now 4:30.

00:45:01.776 --> 00:45:02.643 position:63%
Look at this.

00:45:08.139 --> 00:45:09.907 position:63%
-This is your ranch, Joel?
-Yes, sir.

00:45:09.974 --> 00:45:11.369 position:63%
-How many acres?

00:45:11.436 --> 00:45:14.778 position:63%
About 1,400 deeded
and then about 700 leased.

00:45:14.845 --> 00:45:17.282 position:63%
-Wow. Big cattle, horse operation?

00:45:17.349 --> 00:45:19.050 position:63%
-A lot of ground, yeah.
-Okay.

00:45:19.117 --> 00:45:21.919 position:63%
-So this is your full livelihood?
-This is it, yeah.

00:45:21.986 --> 00:45:24.623 position:63%
-And look at that view.
You ever get sick of that?

00:45:24.690 --> 00:45:27.358 position:63%
-Never. Never.
And it changes every minute.

00:45:27.425 --> 00:45:31.492 position:63%
-Where I just came from, Chesaw,
that's your local social gathering place.

00:45:31.559 --> 00:45:32.396 position:63%
Yeah.

00:45:32.463 --> 00:45:35.800 position:63%
-And that's what? 30 minutes?
-40.

00:45:35.867 --> 00:45:36.767 position:63%
-40?
-Yeah.

00:45:37.268 --> 00:45:39.037 position:63%
And it's about 45 to Republic.

00:45:39.104 --> 00:45:42.094 position:63%
And Wauconda's dead. So nothing there.

00:45:42.773 --> 00:45:45.150 position:63%
-All right. Let's cruise.
-Okay.

00:45:45.217 --> 00:45:47.745 position:63%
[Peter chuckles] What kind of dog is this?

00:45:47.812 --> 00:45:51.148 position:63%
-Central Asian Shepherd.
That's my wolf welcoming committee.

00:45:51.215 --> 00:45:53.350 position:63%
-You have wolves out here?
-Yeah.

00:45:53.417 --> 00:45:54.785 position:63%
-Seriously?
-Yeah.

00:45:54.852 --> 00:45:57.755 position:63%
-Is that a problem?
-If you don't catch 'em, yeah.

00:45:57.822 --> 00:46:00.758 position:63%
They would probably kill him,
but he'd put up a good fight.

00:46:00.825 --> 00:46:02.199 position:63%
-Oh, they would kill him?

00:46:02.266 --> 00:46:04.374 position:63%
-Well…
-Look how huge his head is.

00:46:04.441 --> 00:46:07.099 position:63%
He could probably take one pretty good.

00:46:07.166 --> 00:46:11.502 position:63%
They've got a bite pressure of 6,500 PSI,

00:46:11.569 --> 00:46:14.036 position:63%
which is in the same realm as a wolf.

00:46:14.490 --> 00:46:15.323 position:63%
-Okay.

00:46:15.390 --> 00:46:18.742 position:63%
And they're bred… They're from countries
that end with stan mostly.

00:46:18.809 --> 00:46:20.811 position:63%
That's what they're bred for,
for a thousand years,

00:46:20.878 --> 00:46:22.878 position:63%
is taking care of their herds.

00:46:22.947 --> 00:46:24.648 position:63%
So he's a deterrent.

00:46:24.715 --> 00:46:26.884 position:63%
Whether he can stop them or not,
I don't know.

00:46:26.951 --> 00:46:28.886 position:63%
-So have you seen them?
-Oh, yeah.

00:46:28.953 --> 00:46:30.988 position:63%
They're around.
You know, you'll hear them howling.

00:46:31.055 --> 00:46:32.089 position:63%
They'll even answer you.

00:46:32.156 --> 00:46:35.026 position:63%
So coyote's not really a big issue,
but wolves, right?

00:46:35.093 --> 00:46:38.163 position:63%
Not a big issue. The coyote,
if you got a bunch of chickens or sheep,

00:46:38.230 --> 00:46:40.349 position:63%
that kind of thing, you got problems.

00:46:40.416 --> 00:46:43.452 position:63%
But they don't bother
the horses or the cows.

00:46:44.627 --> 00:46:47.664 position:63%
-But the wolves…
-But the wolves do and the lions are bad.

00:46:47.731 --> 00:46:51.842 position:63%
We lost eight colts last year the lions
and then two more tore up

00:46:51.909 --> 00:46:53.345 position:63%
that we had to put down eventually.

00:46:53.412 --> 00:46:54.245 position:63%
Wow.

00:46:54.312 --> 00:46:56.836 position:63%
That's a big hit,
you know, on a small ranch

00:46:56.903 --> 00:47:00.624 position:63%
in the vicinity of $50,000,
$60,000 worth of colts.

00:47:00.691 --> 00:47:02.721 position:63%
That's what I do,
I sell colts for a living.

00:47:02.788 --> 00:47:04.488 position:63%
-It's that much?
-Yeah.

00:47:05.289 --> 00:47:07.902 position:63%
We've been here since 1990.

00:47:08.993 --> 00:47:12.830 position:63%
Haven't made a dime the whole time,
but I would do it again.

00:47:12.897 --> 00:47:14.898 position:63%
-You're just flowing.
You're covering your costs.

00:47:14.965 --> 00:47:16.800 position:63%
We're squeaking by, yeah.

00:47:16.867 --> 00:47:22.806 position:63%
We got a lot of foals this year
and sold more than we ever have.

00:47:22.873 --> 00:47:26.176 position:63%
But you also,
then you look at diesel prices, you know,

00:47:26.243 --> 00:47:28.612 position:63%
it's gonna be
another break-even thing I think.

00:47:28.679 --> 00:47:30.682 position:63%
-Okay, so are you selling these horses?

00:47:30.749 --> 00:47:33.084 position:63%
-There's a few still out here,
a few babies,

00:47:33.151 --> 00:47:35.318 position:63%
but most of these are broodmares.

00:47:35.385 --> 00:47:38.745 position:63%
There's a mare and a foal right there.
That one's for sale.

00:47:38.812 --> 00:47:42.192 position:63%
We usually sell them after we wean them
when they're about five or six months old.

00:47:42.259 --> 00:47:46.230 position:63%
And then they go all over the country
and even some to Europe.

00:47:46.844 --> 00:47:48.353 position:63%
-That's an old homesteader's cabin?

00:47:48.420 --> 00:47:51.635 position:63%
-Yeah. Fred Schmilling was his name
and he did a little bit of everything.

00:47:51.702 --> 00:47:55.868 position:63%
He had a few cows and goats
and chickens, sold eggs.

00:47:56.607 --> 00:47:58.407 position:63%
Prospected, had a few cows.

00:47:58.910 --> 00:48:02.030 position:63%
But his main money crop was moonshine.

00:48:02.447 --> 00:48:03.580 position:63%
-Okay, right.

00:48:03.647 --> 00:48:07.951 position:63%
And he had horses. He had a big herd
of horses up here, and he had a stud.

00:48:08.018 --> 00:48:09.853 position:63%
Everybody came to breed to him.

00:48:09.920 --> 00:48:13.924 position:63%
But he kept those horses for pack horses
and he would load them up with whiskey

00:48:13.991 --> 00:48:18.262 position:63%
and head out to the highway and meet
a car going to Wenatchee or Spokane.

00:48:18.329 --> 00:48:19.796 position:63%
That's how he stayed afloat.

00:48:19.863 --> 00:48:23.701 position:63%
Or he would go to Canada and get it.
It's about 17 miles.

00:48:23.768 --> 00:48:25.669 position:63%
And get whiskey up there
and bring it back down.

00:48:25.736 --> 00:48:27.450 position:63%
-Yeah, I was just at the border.
-Yeah.

00:48:27.517 --> 00:48:28.840 position:63%
-Near Molson.
-Mm-hmm.

00:48:28.907 --> 00:48:31.241 position:63%
It's just a barbed wire fence.

00:48:31.308 --> 00:48:33.912 position:63%
Is there any flow going over the border
up in these parts?

00:48:33.979 --> 00:48:36.643 position:63%
Oh, there used to be
a lot of cross-border stuff.

00:48:36.710 --> 00:48:38.951 position:63%
There's not so much anymore just 'cause

00:48:39.018 --> 00:48:41.652 position:63%
gotta be such a hassle
going across I think.

00:48:41.719 --> 00:48:44.055 position:63%
-Right. But I'm talking illegal stuff.

00:48:44.122 --> 00:48:45.838 position:63%
Oh, yeah. [laughs]

00:48:46.525 --> 00:48:49.693 position:63%
There's dope coming across,
and they're smuggling people,

00:48:49.760 --> 00:48:52.096 position:63%
all that kind of…
Everything that's ill in the world

00:48:52.163 --> 00:48:54.798 position:63%
comes across that border,
it seems like, you know.

00:48:54.865 --> 00:48:57.468 position:63%
It's so wide open and sparse up here

00:48:57.535 --> 00:48:59.510 position:63%
that there's no way
you can guard the whole thing.

00:48:59.577 --> 00:49:02.975 position:63%
-Yeah, I haven't seen any Customs
and Border Protection trucks

00:49:03.042 --> 00:49:04.041 position:63%
where I was at.

00:49:04.108 --> 00:49:06.284 position:63%
They're around, but, you know,

00:49:06.351 --> 00:49:09.213 position:63%
I mean, you may go two weeks
without seeing one, you know.

00:49:09.280 --> 00:49:13.050 position:63%
-Right. So how's the economics
of this work these days, ranching?

00:49:13.117 --> 00:49:14.622 position:63%
-Horrible.
-Horrible?

00:49:15.319 --> 00:49:18.455 position:63%
Nobody does it to make money
that I know of.

00:49:18.522 --> 00:49:22.593 position:63%
Everybody here just likes the lifestyle,
likes the animals, likes the country.

00:49:22.660 --> 00:49:23.827 position:63%
It gets a hold of you.

00:49:23.894 --> 00:49:28.265 position:63%
Well, I couldn't do anything else.
You know, I've had enough of people.

00:49:28.332 --> 00:49:29.628 position:63%
Further away, I could keep…

00:49:29.695 --> 00:49:32.202 position:63%
I mean, I did my time in Olympia
for 20 years, you know.

00:49:32.269 --> 00:49:35.906 position:63%
-Okay, okay, so we should let
the audience know your background was?

00:49:35.973 --> 00:49:39.176 position:63%
I was in the House of Representatives
for 20 years in Olympia.

00:49:39.243 --> 00:49:40.779 position:63%
-Okay.
-Washington State Legislature.

00:49:40.846 --> 00:49:43.379 position:63%
-Okay, so you're in government?
-I was.

00:49:44.116 --> 00:49:46.283 position:63%
I don't claim that as a, you know…

00:49:46.350 --> 00:49:49.186 position:63%
I don't consider myself
much of a politician.

00:49:49.253 --> 00:49:51.054 position:63%
-You don't miss it?
-Nah.

00:49:51.121 --> 00:49:54.391 position:63%
-See, this is an area we've been doing
some forest restoration.

00:49:54.458 --> 00:49:56.894 position:63%
The forest that was coming in
was just too thick.

00:49:56.961 --> 00:49:59.263 position:63%
There's too many fir trees.
It should be pine and larch.

00:49:59.330 --> 00:50:03.167 position:63%
And we're trying to convert it
back toward a more natural forest.

00:50:03.234 --> 00:50:05.002 position:63%
And then they're more fire resilient.

00:50:05.069 --> 00:50:05.903 position:63%
-Okay, yeah.

00:50:05.970 --> 00:50:08.906 position:63%
Like this, a fire can go through,
it's gonna burn the grass.

00:50:08.973 --> 00:50:10.974 position:63%
Unless it's really blowing,
it's not going to go up

00:50:11.041 --> 00:50:12.509 position:63%
and take the whole forest.

00:50:12.576 --> 00:50:14.011 position:63%
Most trees will live through it.

00:50:14.078 --> 00:50:15.846 position:63%
-So you're thinning this out?
-Yep.

00:50:15.913 --> 00:50:19.516 position:63%
-I was on the Mescalero Apache Tribe
in New Mexico.

00:50:19.583 --> 00:50:22.553 position:63%
It looks so beautiful 'cause they
thinned everything out really well.

00:50:22.620 --> 00:50:25.122 position:63%
Same thing on the Colville Reservation
right up here.

00:50:25.189 --> 00:50:26.957 position:63%
They're doing some beautiful forestry work

00:50:27.024 --> 00:50:30.829 position:63%
and their forests are a lot more resilient
when the fires do come.

00:50:30.896 --> 00:50:35.096 position:63%
-Right. I tapped in for a few minutes
today to the Colville Res.

00:50:35.733 --> 00:50:38.837 position:63%
Every res has a different story,
different vibe to some degree.

00:50:38.904 --> 00:50:42.738 position:63%
-They do.
-But that's 12 tribes coming in one place.

00:50:42.805 --> 00:50:45.205 position:63%
Their elections are pretty exciting.

00:50:46.143 --> 00:50:47.145 position:63%
It's volatile.

00:50:47.212 --> 00:50:51.448 position:63%
You know, when I was in the legislature,
you try and build up relationships

00:50:51.515 --> 00:50:53.517 position:63%
with people on the tribal council.

00:50:53.584 --> 00:50:56.220 position:63%
You know, you help them out
when you can too.

00:50:56.287 --> 00:50:59.590 position:63%
The problem was the turnover was so big,
you just get to know somebody

00:50:59.657 --> 00:51:01.425 position:63%
and then they get bounced, you know.

00:51:01.492 --> 00:51:03.961 position:63%
-That's common?
-Yeah, it's real common.

00:51:04.028 --> 00:51:07.631 position:63%
There's old family rivalries,
and intertribal rivalries,

00:51:07.698 --> 00:51:09.900 position:63%
and 12 tribe rivalries.

00:51:09.967 --> 00:51:11.871 position:63%
So it's, it's… [chuckles]

00:51:11.938 --> 00:51:14.271 position:63%
There's not a lot of continuity
on the res.

00:51:14.838 --> 00:51:16.373 position:63%
This will be our winter range.

00:51:16.440 --> 00:51:18.942 position:63%
Horses can make it out here in the winter.

00:51:19.009 --> 00:51:22.146 position:63%
As long as there's grass,
even if there's two, three feet of snow,

00:51:22.213 --> 00:51:23.780 position:63%
they'll paw.

00:51:23.847 --> 00:51:26.165 position:63%
And they actually
do better out here sometimes

00:51:26.232 --> 00:51:29.953 position:63%
than the ones I got in the corral
that I'm feeding all winter.

00:51:30.020 --> 00:51:33.980 position:63%
This is what I enjoy, going out
and checking horses and seeing the country

00:51:34.047 --> 00:51:37.928 position:63%
and figuring out what I gotta do
this winter to make things work.

00:51:37.995 --> 00:51:39.363 position:63%
That's what's interesting to me.

00:51:39.430 --> 00:51:42.782 position:63%
And and I don't really miss
being around a lot of people.

00:51:42.849 --> 00:51:46.570 position:63%
I don't miss staying in Olympia
for four or five months a year.

00:51:46.637 --> 00:51:48.071 position:63%
This is what I love.

00:51:48.138 --> 00:51:50.807 position:63%
-So your biggest threat out here is fire.

00:51:50.874 --> 00:51:53.644 position:63%
-It's…
-Fire, wolves, taxes, in that order?

00:51:53.711 --> 00:51:58.849 position:63%
Fire, wolves, taxes and new regulations,
and right now the price of diesel.

00:51:58.916 --> 00:52:00.784 position:63%
-Okay, what's diesel doing
to you ranchers?

00:52:00.851 --> 00:52:02.786 position:63%
I'm sure that's talk of the town, right?

00:52:02.853 --> 00:52:06.422 position:63%
Oh, yeah. It's a lot of people
are pulling back on what they do.

00:52:06.489 --> 00:52:08.325 position:63%
Here's kind of a cool thing.

00:52:08.392 --> 00:52:10.355 position:63%
-Lightning hit that.
-Oh, that is… Oh, no way.

00:52:10.422 --> 00:52:12.862 position:63%
-Probably a hundred years ago,
and it corkscrews down the tree

00:52:12.929 --> 00:52:14.531 position:63%
and blew all the bark off.

00:52:14.598 --> 00:52:17.065 position:63%
And the tough old thing stayed alive.

00:52:17.601 --> 00:52:19.002 position:63%
-That is so cool.

00:52:19.069 --> 00:52:21.471 position:63%
-But the diesel, yeah,
everybody's just cutting back.

00:52:21.538 --> 00:52:24.168 position:63%
You're not doing a single thing
you don't have to do.

00:52:24.235 --> 00:52:27.477 position:63%
When I'm running my tractor,
when I have to do it to feed,

00:52:27.544 --> 00:52:29.980 position:63%
I just keep it in an idle, you know?

00:52:30.047 --> 00:52:33.083 position:63%
I mean, you just…
you're so fuel conscious.

00:52:33.150 --> 00:52:35.419 position:63%
And I finally went down
and filled my tank the other day.

00:52:35.486 --> 00:52:38.221 position:63%
And you know, it's what should have been
three, four hundred is,

00:52:38.288 --> 00:52:40.191 position:63%
you know, 750 bucks, you know?

00:52:40.258 --> 00:52:43.660 position:63%
So a lot of people aren't even
planting a crop because they can't afford

00:52:43.727 --> 00:52:45.262 position:63%
to take the tractors into the field.

00:52:45.329 --> 00:52:46.729 position:63%
-Seriously?
-Oh, yeah.

00:52:47.369 --> 00:52:48.832 position:63%
People are pulling out orchards.

00:52:48.899 --> 00:52:53.904 position:63%
You know, that's not just diesel.
That's a whole run of different things.

00:52:53.971 --> 00:52:57.474 position:63%
Washington in particular
does not value agriculture.

00:52:57.541 --> 00:53:00.408 position:63%
You know, you go across the line
up into BC…

00:53:01.979 --> 00:53:04.014 position:63%
And here, this looks like
a third world country.

00:53:04.081 --> 00:53:06.151 position:63%
You cross that line
and it looks prosperous.

00:53:06.218 --> 00:53:08.452 position:63%
They've got nice buildings,
nice equipment.

00:53:08.519 --> 00:53:09.886 position:63%
Everything looks good.

00:53:09.953 --> 00:53:15.230 position:63%
Because their tax code rewards investment,
whereas our tax code punishes it.

00:53:15.859 --> 00:53:20.565 position:63%
Canada's only got a little bit of farmland
up there, and they revere it.

00:53:20.632 --> 00:53:23.767 position:63%
And here we take it for granted
and we're losing farms every day.

00:53:23.834 --> 00:53:26.703 position:63%
-They reward investing
in farming and agriculture?

00:53:26.770 --> 00:53:27.604 position:63%
Absolutely, yeah.

00:53:27.671 --> 00:53:29.976 position:63%
-We have so much of it,
we take it for granted.

00:53:30.043 --> 00:53:31.531 position:63%
-I think so. We're lucky.

00:53:31.598 --> 00:53:33.331 position:63%
We've got a lot of
good agricultural ground,

00:53:33.398 --> 00:53:35.380 position:63%
but we're losing it awful fast.

00:53:35.447 --> 00:53:37.380 position:63%
There's one pony. Hey, baby.

00:53:38.427 --> 00:53:41.787 position:63%
[whistles]

00:53:43.053 --> 00:53:46.253 position:63%
She's gonna be p*ssed
if I don't have some grain.

00:53:48.358 --> 00:53:51.094 position:63%
-So you build a relationship
with all your animals?

00:53:51.161 --> 00:53:51.995 position:63%
Oh, yeah.

00:53:52.730 --> 00:53:56.063 position:63%
-So me as a consumer,
I'm gonna get a steak dinner.

00:53:56.633 --> 00:53:58.835 position:63%
Does it perhaps come from your ranch?

00:53:58.902 --> 00:54:02.475 position:63%
No, I don't sell…
I've got very few cattle anymore.

00:54:02.542 --> 00:54:07.210 position:63%
This ranch is better suited for horses,
and we kinda had cattle in the early '90s,

00:54:07.277 --> 00:54:11.415 position:63%
and then kept dropping the numbers on
the cattle and raising the horse numbers.

00:54:11.482 --> 00:54:12.883 position:63%
That's what we've got.

00:54:12.950 --> 00:54:15.419 position:63%
We've got about 140 head
of horses here right now.

00:54:15.486 --> 00:54:18.321 position:63%
-So you don't do cattle,
but I passed a lot of cattle today.

00:54:18.388 --> 00:54:22.492 position:63%
Well, I've got some down in the canyon,
and we just raise it for our own food.

00:54:22.559 --> 00:54:24.494 position:63%
And then we sell a few here and there.

00:54:24.561 --> 00:54:27.931 position:63%
-But the farms, the ranches
I passed today with all the cattle,

00:54:27.998 --> 00:54:29.574 position:63%
where does that beef go?

00:54:29.641 --> 00:54:31.101 position:63%
Well, some of it goes local.

00:54:31.168 --> 00:54:33.870 position:63%
You know, it's something
that we've worked hard on

00:54:33.937 --> 00:54:38.341 position:63%
is getting the local producer
connected with the local customers.

00:54:38.408 --> 00:54:43.113 position:63%
And there's been a lot of problems of how
do you get it USDA certified and all that.

00:54:43.180 --> 00:54:44.848 position:63%
We made some progress to where

00:54:44.915 --> 00:54:49.186 position:63%
there's a few butcher places around here
where you can get it USDA certified.

00:54:49.253 --> 00:54:51.221 position:63%
Then you can sell to the open market.

00:54:51.288 --> 00:54:54.191 position:63%
A lot of people just…
They'll sell a live animal to a neighbor,

00:54:54.258 --> 00:54:55.711 position:63%
and they'll take it and butcher it.

00:54:55.778 --> 00:55:00.522 position:63%
But some of it goes down to Agri Beef
down in the basin.

00:55:01.665 --> 00:55:05.969 position:63%
You know, one of the gigantic packing
houses, although they're hanging it up.

00:55:06.036 --> 00:55:08.338 position:63%
So we don't know where all the cattle
are going to go.

00:55:08.405 --> 00:55:11.141 position:63%
-They're hanging it up, why?
-They're going to… Well too high a cost.

00:55:11.208 --> 00:55:12.309 position:63%
They're not making money.

00:55:12.376 --> 00:55:15.390 position:63%
Diesel prices have hurt them,
labor prices,

00:55:15.457 --> 00:55:17.680 position:63%
tax structure in the state of Washington.

00:55:17.747 --> 00:55:21.785 position:63%
They've got the place for sale right now,
but I hope they don't just leave.

00:55:21.852 --> 00:55:24.121 position:63%
You know, I hope somebody buys it.

00:55:24.688 --> 00:55:27.695 position:63%
-[Horse snorts]
-[Joel] Hey, Darlin'.

00:55:28.859 --> 00:55:31.094 position:63%
-Your son is in Idaho drilling for gold?

00:55:31.161 --> 00:55:33.111 position:63%
-Right.
-He's mining gold?

00:55:33.178 --> 00:55:36.005 position:63%
Well, he's working for
an exploration outfit.

00:55:36.072 --> 00:55:36.905 position:63%
-Okay.

00:55:36.972 --> 00:55:40.470 position:63%
There's some gold mines over there
and they're drilling the surrounding area.

00:55:40.537 --> 00:55:43.954 position:63%
They'll drill down maybe
several thousand feet,

00:55:44.540 --> 00:55:46.810 position:63%
and then they check
each section as they go.

00:55:46.877 --> 00:55:51.944 position:63%
And if they hit gold content, then it's
a chance they'll put a mine in there.

00:55:52.416 --> 00:55:53.252 position:63%
-Right.

00:55:53.319 --> 00:55:57.587 position:63%
So what's the overall economic health
of this part of the state right now?

00:55:57.654 --> 00:55:59.502 position:63%
-Dismal.
-Dismal?

00:56:00.457 --> 00:56:02.626 position:63%
Yeah, this is mineral-rich area.

00:56:02.693 --> 00:56:06.431 position:63%
There was a bunch of… There's three mines
on this ranch, you know, old mines.

00:56:06.498 --> 00:56:09.833 position:63%
And Republic was founded on that.

00:56:09.900 --> 00:56:13.170 position:63%
The two biggest gold mines in the state
were just a few miles over here.

00:56:13.237 --> 00:56:16.985 position:63%
-Republic, a town not far away?
-Town east of here, about 15 miles east.

00:56:17.052 --> 00:56:19.342 position:63%
-Okay.
-I think two mines right in the town.

00:56:19.409 --> 00:56:22.445 position:63%
And that's been the culture
for a hundred and some years

00:56:22.512 --> 00:56:26.984 position:63%
is mining and timber and agriculture.

00:56:27.051 --> 00:56:29.753 position:63%
None of those are really valued much
by the government right now,

00:56:29.820 --> 00:56:32.207 position:63%
so they've just unbelievable regulations.

00:56:32.274 --> 00:56:35.358 position:63%
-But know what's great about electric cars
is you don't need to mine.

00:56:35.425 --> 00:56:37.479 position:63%
You don't need any copper.

00:56:37.546 --> 00:56:38.821 position:63%
[laughter]

00:56:38.894 --> 00:56:41.417 position:63%
Yeah, don't look too close
at the environmental cost

00:56:41.484 --> 00:56:44.367 position:63%
of electric cars or windmills, either one.

00:56:44.434 --> 00:56:48.121 position:63%
-Yeah. How many tons of steel
do you need for a windmill?

00:56:48.188 --> 00:56:50.574 position:63%
It's unbelievable.
And the foundation, the cement.

00:56:50.641 --> 00:56:53.276 position:63%
-So there's no easy way for energy, right?

00:56:53.343 --> 00:56:55.988 position:63%
There's no cheap,
no easy way, you know.

00:56:56.055 --> 00:56:57.675 position:63%
And it's like,

00:56:57.742 --> 00:57:00.624 position:63%
there's a lot of positive things
about some of the alternative energy.

00:57:00.691 --> 00:57:03.286 position:63%
-The house there is solar, you know.
-Awesome.

00:57:03.353 --> 00:57:05.339 position:63%
But they've come a long ways
with the technology.

00:57:05.406 --> 00:57:07.224 position:63%
I wouldn't have done it 20 years ago.

00:57:07.291 --> 00:57:11.461 position:63%
-Yeah, solar I'm a big fan of
when it's in areas like top of buildings

00:57:11.528 --> 00:57:12.662 position:63%
or in the desert.

00:57:12.729 --> 00:57:15.866 position:63%
When it takes over ag land,
it just doesn't feel good.

00:57:15.933 --> 00:57:18.475 position:63%
It's insane.
You're saving the world by doing that?

00:57:18.542 --> 00:57:22.306 position:63%
Yeah. The windmills,
I'm not a big fan of because

00:57:23.491 --> 00:57:27.067 position:63%
in environments like this,
you're here for the nature.

00:57:27.533 --> 00:57:29.546 position:63%
Windmills,
they're blinking lights all night.

00:57:29.613 --> 00:57:30.580 position:63%
-Oh, I know.

00:57:30.647 --> 00:57:33.116 position:63%
And depending how close you are,
they make noise.

00:57:33.183 --> 00:57:36.319 position:63%
And you have to love mining.
If you want wind, you have to love mining.

00:57:36.386 --> 00:57:39.586 position:63%
-You absolutely do.
-So everything's a trade-off.

00:57:40.290 --> 00:57:42.759 position:63%
Small-scale nuclear I think
is the way forward.

00:57:42.826 --> 00:57:45.262 position:63%
-I do too.
-Natural gas and solar.

00:57:45.329 --> 00:57:46.863 position:63%
-I agree.
-But what do I know?

00:57:46.930 --> 00:57:49.165 position:63%
I'm just observing what I see
in the country.

00:57:49.232 --> 00:57:51.836 position:63%
-Well, you're being
too common sense I think.

00:57:51.903 --> 00:57:55.972 position:63%
Okay, so what do Americans need to know,
or anyone who's interested need to know

00:57:56.039 --> 00:57:58.839 position:63%
about ranching
and like what do we not get?

00:57:59.376 --> 00:58:03.280 position:63%
Well, if this was about money,
what I would do is I'd bust this up

00:58:03.347 --> 00:58:06.727 position:63%
into 20-acre ranchettes,
and we'd sell it to somebody.

00:58:06.794 --> 00:58:09.619 position:63%
And you've got a cabin on every 20 acres,

00:58:09.686 --> 00:58:13.723 position:63%
and there's usually 10 or 15 dead cars
sitting there and a bunch of goats

00:58:13.790 --> 00:58:17.560 position:63%
and wolf dogs, and that,
that's what we get when that happens.

00:58:17.627 --> 00:58:20.935 position:63%
There's been a bunch of ranches here
busted up into 20-acre ranchettes.

00:58:21.002 --> 00:58:21.835 position:63%
-Okay.

00:58:21.902 --> 00:58:23.496 position:63%
And then you lose all the habitat.

00:58:23.563 --> 00:58:25.869 position:63%
I provide a lot of habitat
right here for wildlife.

00:58:25.936 --> 00:58:29.072 position:63%
You know, they're welcome here.
We've got water troughs.

00:58:29.139 --> 00:58:33.143 position:63%
You know, we help the wildlife a lot
unless they start messing with us.

00:58:33.210 --> 00:58:34.923 position:63%
And then I'm going to mess back

00:58:34.990 --> 00:58:38.481 position:63%
when it comes to wolves or lions,
large predators.

00:58:38.548 --> 00:58:41.351 position:63%
That's basically the two choices.

00:58:41.418 --> 00:58:45.790 position:63%
You know, you keep this in agriculture.
We're raising horses and cows here.

00:58:45.857 --> 00:58:49.292 position:63%
And the other choice
is 20-acre ranchettes,

00:58:49.359 --> 00:58:51.928 position:63%
and then there's
the habitat's lost forever.

00:58:51.995 --> 00:58:53.128 position:63%
It gets degraded.

00:58:53.897 --> 00:58:56.800 position:63%
-So after working in government,
being out here,

00:58:56.867 --> 00:58:58.435 position:63%
I don't want to get you going too much.

00:58:58.502 --> 00:59:00.203 position:63%
Like you said, I started to get you going.

00:59:00.270 --> 00:59:04.174 position:63%
But I feel like 80% of the country
is mostly in the same

00:59:04.241 --> 00:59:06.109 position:63%
wavelength with most things.

00:59:06.176 --> 00:59:08.345 position:63%
And then it's the fringes on both sides.

00:59:08.412 --> 00:59:11.548 position:63%
-Totally.
-Firing weapons at each other, right?

00:59:11.615 --> 00:59:14.684 position:63%
-And that's what makes it…
-And bringing us along for it all.

00:59:14.751 --> 00:59:19.022 position:63%
Yeah, yeah, you don't see anything
in the news about any of the normal people

00:59:19.089 --> 00:59:21.358 position:63%
or people trying to do good things
I don't think.

00:59:21.425 --> 00:59:23.426 position:63%
It's got to be something
controversial and nasty.

00:59:23.493 --> 00:59:25.095 position:63%
And that's what they cater to.

00:59:25.162 --> 00:59:27.430 position:63%
The news, I don't even try
and watch it anymore.

00:59:27.497 --> 00:59:30.033 position:63%
It's turned into
how many clicks can I get.

00:59:30.100 --> 00:59:32.900 position:63%
You know, and that's a horrible motivator.

00:59:33.470 --> 00:59:37.303 position:63%
Yeah, but this channel
and this amazing audience out there

00:59:37.874 --> 00:59:39.442 position:63%
is bucking that trend.

00:59:39.509 --> 00:59:41.611 position:63%
This is not a clickbaity video at all.

00:59:41.678 --> 00:59:44.014 position:63%
-No, no. I can see that.
-It's slow burn.

00:59:44.081 --> 00:59:46.516 position:63%
-Yeah.
-It's eating your vegetables a little bit.

00:59:46.583 --> 00:59:50.983 position:63%
Well, not much for vegetables,
but if you want a steak, we'll talk.

00:59:51.050 --> 00:59:52.369 position:63%
[laughter]

00:59:52.436 --> 00:59:56.526 position:63%
-But what I'm saying is it's not like
a dopamine-driven party the whole time,

00:59:56.593 --> 00:59:58.228 position:63%
but you'll learn something by the end.

00:59:58.295 --> 01:00:00.327 position:63%
-I think so, yeah.
You're going to see a world that

01:00:00.394 --> 01:00:02.300 position:63%
most people don't know is out there.

01:00:02.367 --> 01:00:06.608 position:63%
-Why do you think most people don't know
this part of Washington exists?

01:00:06.675 --> 01:00:07.839 position:63%
Um…

01:00:07.906 --> 01:00:10.607 position:63%
Well, Gary Locke, former governor,
I think said it best.

01:00:10.674 --> 01:00:12.942 position:63%
He said,
"Eastern Washington has one function,

01:00:13.009 --> 01:00:16.112 position:63%
and that's as a playground
for Western Washington."

01:00:16.179 --> 01:00:17.631 position:63%
You know, he actually said that.

01:00:17.698 --> 01:00:21.929 position:63%
And so we're viewed by the majority
of the population on the coast as just,

01:00:21.996 --> 01:00:25.129 position:63%
you know, maybe we can go over there
and go water skiing or,

01:00:25.196 --> 01:00:26.990 position:63%
you know, do some recreational thing.

01:00:27.057 --> 01:00:28.691 position:63%
But they don't take us seriously.

01:00:29.337 --> 01:00:31.793 position:63%
-You know what? Can I push back
even though I'm not from here?

01:00:31.860 --> 01:00:33.897 position:63%
-Sure.
-And you might kick me out of your ranch.

01:00:33.964 --> 01:00:37.100 position:63%
-Push away.
But it's a long ways to the gate.

01:00:37.167 --> 01:00:38.935 position:63%
It is a long ways to the gate.

01:00:39.002 --> 01:00:42.004 position:63%
Western Washington isn't even one thing.

01:00:42.071 --> 01:00:44.192 position:63%
Because I was out on the peninsula.

01:00:44.259 --> 01:00:47.077 position:63%
-You're totally right.
It's urban rural is what it is.

01:00:47.144 --> 01:00:49.145 position:63%
-It's urban rural.
-It's urban rural completely.

01:00:49.212 --> 01:00:51.614 position:63%
Forks, I went out there when I was 16,

01:00:51.681 --> 01:00:55.019 position:63%
logging out there in the boom years
in the early '70s.

01:00:55.086 --> 01:00:57.487 position:63%
And there's no different, Forks.

01:00:57.554 --> 01:01:01.524 position:63%
The closeness of that community
was the same way out there as it is here.

01:01:01.591 --> 01:01:03.091 position:63%
But it's not celebrated.

01:01:03.158 --> 01:01:04.838 position:63%
You know, Warren Magnuson used to say,

01:01:04.905 --> 01:01:07.698 position:63%
"I can stand in the Space Needle
and get all the votes I need."

01:01:07.765 --> 01:01:09.899 position:63%
And he's just about right.

01:01:09.966 --> 01:01:13.203 position:63%
I don't think they understand us.
We don't understand them.

01:01:13.270 --> 01:01:15.305 position:63%
And nobody really tries to
bridge the middle.

01:01:15.372 --> 01:01:17.474 position:63%
I think the best work I did
in the legislature was

01:01:17.541 --> 01:01:21.355 position:63%
bringing urban legislators
right out here, right like this.

01:01:21.429 --> 01:01:24.436 position:63%
And all of a sudden, they kind of
comprehend that I'm not trying to

01:01:24.503 --> 01:01:26.984 position:63%
destroy the environment
or anything else.

01:01:27.051 --> 01:01:29.786 position:63%
They had a whole new appreciation
for what we deal with over here.

01:01:29.853 --> 01:01:31.088 position:63%
But how many have seen it?

01:01:31.155 --> 01:01:32.756 position:63%
-It's just you need face time.

01:01:32.823 --> 01:01:35.626 position:63%
-That's the only thing that works.
-That's the only thing that works.

01:01:35.693 --> 01:01:38.595 position:63%
-And then you also realize rural America

01:01:38.662 --> 01:01:41.598 position:63%
is dependent on urban America
and vice versa.

01:01:42.199 --> 01:01:44.101 position:63%
As long as you put in
the vice versa, yeah.

01:01:44.168 --> 01:01:46.894 position:63%
Urban America needs what you provide.

01:01:46.961 --> 01:01:48.789 position:63%
Let's say a cattle ranch, right?

01:01:48.856 --> 01:01:50.647 position:63%
Or whatever, the food supply.

01:01:50.714 --> 01:01:54.673 position:63%
And then you want this technology
that usually comes from cities.

01:01:54.740 --> 01:01:58.007 position:63%
It usually does. Yeah. There is a balance.

01:01:58.074 --> 01:02:00.583 position:63%
-So it's like we're
indirectly connected every day.

01:02:00.650 --> 01:02:03.920 position:63%
The mindset is that we're just
recreation land for Western Washington.

01:02:03.987 --> 01:02:05.187 position:63%
I mean, that is a…

01:02:06.110 --> 01:02:07.377 position:63%
That's a screwed-up thought.

01:02:07.444 --> 01:02:08.716 position:63%
-That gets to you a little bit?

01:02:08.783 --> 01:02:11.454 position:63%
Well, yeah. We put a lot into
